PG.notes("l359", "<p class=\"summary\">Project overview for OpenD, an OpenSea-style NFT marketplace on the Internet Computer. Explains what fungibility means, how real NFTs reference off-chain storage, and the features the finished app will have.</p>\n<h3>Fungible vs non-fungible</h3>\n<ul><li>Fungible assets are interchangeable: any banknote of a given value can be swapped for another with no loss. The coins/tokens built earlier (Bitcoin, Ethereum, the course's DANG token) behave this way.</li><li>A non-fungible token (NFT) has a unique identifier and unique data, so no two are equivalent, like an original painting versus a perfect copy.</li><li>NFTs most often represent digital images; ownership is recorded on a blockchain.</li></ul>\n<h3>Marketplace context</h3>\n<ul><li>OpenSea is the model: users list NFTs and others buy them, prices quoted in a crypto currency (ETH there), and the platform keeps a small percentage fee per sale (about 2.5%).</li><li>Takeaway for developers: running the marketplace platform can be more lucrative than being one creator.</li><li>Large mainstream sales (an auction-house sale of a digital art collection for tens of millions of dollars) show demand.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where NFT data usually lives</h3>\n<ul><li>On most chains the token is a smart contract that stores a token URI pointing to the image, commonly on IPFS or even a company server. The image itself is not on-chain.</li><li>The demo follows an OpenSea item: its contract address leads to Etherscan, the contract exposes a token URI function, and passing the token id returns an IPFS identifier that resolves to a publicly accessible image URL.</li><li>In OpenD the image bytes are stored on-chain inside the Internet Computer canister, so the asset does not depend on an outside host.</li></ul>\n<h3>What OpenD will do</h3>\n<ul><li>Inspired by CryptoPunks pixel-art characters. The course supplies six pixel-art images (drawn by the instructor, downloadable from the lecture resources) so no purchase is needed.</li><li>Minting: the user uploads an image and a name; this programmatically creates a new canister holding the NFT data (name, owner, image bytes).</li><li>My collection page: shows NFTs the logged-in user owns; each has a Sell button that asks for a price in DANG (the token from the previous project).</li><li>Discover page: lists all NFTs put up for sale; a user cannot buy their own listing but can buy others', paying in DANG, after which the NFT appears in their collection.</li><li>Simplified feature set compared with OpenSea, focusing on core ideas: programmatic canister creation, ownership transfer, and payment using a third-party token.</li><li>Stack: React frontend, Motoko backend on the Internet Computer.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Skills practised: Motoko classes as canisters, actor classes, HashMaps and lists, React Router, authenticated calls from the previous lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Do not assume an NFT's image is on-chain elsewhere: URI-based NFTs can break if the host disappears; that is the weakness OpenD avoids.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l360", "<p class=\"summary\">Starts the OpenD project: downloads the starter, adds a separate NFT canister file registered in dfx.json, converts it from a plain actor to an actor class taking name, owner and image bytes, adds query getters, and mints a first NFT from the command line with dfx deploy --argument.</p>\n<h3>Starter project and layout</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the opend starter zip from the lecture resources, unzip, move it into the projects folder and open in VS Code.</li><li>src contains an almost empty main.mo (the future marketplace backend: listing, buying) and a frontend assets folder with pre-written HTML/CSS; many long class names but each is just an element with styling.</li><li>The learning focus is making backend and frontend cooperate, not the styling.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why the NFT gets its own canister file</h3>\n<ul><li>main.mo is the e-commerce backend. Each NFT, however, is a separately instantiated canister, so its code should not live in main.mo.</li><li>Create src/NFT/nft.mo.</li><li>Register it in dfx.json: add a new entry in the canisters object (placing it right after the first canister entry avoids comma mistakes) with 'main' set to src/NFT/nft.mo and 'type' set to motoko. The canister name used on the CLI is the key, e.g. nft.</li></ul>\n<h3>Smoke test with a plain actor</h3>\n<ul><li>First write actor NFT { Debug.print(&quot;It works.&quot;); } with import Debug from mo:base/Debug.</li><li>Run npm install (installs the many dependencies in package.json, takes a few minutes), then dfx start in one terminal and dfx deploy in another.</li><li>The dfx start terminal should print the message from the nft canister. If not: restart dfx start, check dfx.json syntax, file names and the path.</li></ul>\n<h3>Actor class = a canister factory</h3>\n<ul><li>Add the class keyword: actor class NFT(...) and give it constructor parameters. A plain actor takes no input; an actor class takes arguments when created, which lets code create canisters programmatically from user input (needed for minting later).</li><li>Parameters: name : Text, owner : Principal (requires import Principal from mo:base/Principal), content : [Nat8] (the image as an array of 8-bit natural numbers, i.e. raw bytes).</li><li>Inside the class, copy the parameters into fields: itemName = name, nftOwner = owner, imageBytes = content.</li><li>Every canister instance gets a unique principal id when created, which is what makes each NFT unique and non-interchangeable.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer Motoko versions want the persistent keyword before actor class; the reference repo uses persistent actor class.</li></ul>\n<h3>Query getters</h3>\n<ul><li>public query func getName() : async Text returns itemName.</li><li>getOwner() : async Principal returns nftOwner.</li><li>getAsset() : async [Nat8] returns imageBytes.</li><li>query functions read state quickly without going through consensus; fine for read-only access.</li></ul>\n<h3>Deploying an actor class needs arguments</h3>\n<ul><li>Plain dfx deploy nft now fails with 'Invalid data: Expected arguments, but found none', because the init arguments are required.</li><li>Pass them with --argument followed by a single-quoted Candid tuple: three values separated by commas, a text in double quotes, the owner as principal &quot;...&quot;, and the bytes as vec { ... } of numbers. The README in the lesson supplies the full command with the byte data of a 10x10 pixel hat image.</li><li>Get your own principal with dfx identity get-principal and replace the one in the command, keeping the formatting exactly (quotes after the word principal).</li><li>Run the whole deploy command; it deploys all canisters and hands the argument to the nft one.</li><li>NFT name in the demo: a CryptoDunks title with a number, mimicking OpenSea-style naming.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying</h3>\n<ul><li>dfx canister call nft getName returns the name; getOwner returns the principal; getAsset returns a long list of numbers.</li><li>No parentheses are written after the function name on the CLI.</li><li>The bytes are raw image data and not readable; viewing it requires the frontend, built in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">dfx.json: register the nft canister</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">&quot;nft&quot;: {\n  &quot;main&quot;: &quot;src/NFT/nft.mo&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;motoko&quot;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">src/NFT/nft.mo (final)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\n\nactor class NFT(name : Text, owner : Principal, content : [Nat8]) {\n  let itemName = name;\n  let nftOwner = owner;\n  let imageBytes = content;\n\n  public query func getName() : async Text {\n    return itemName;\n  };\n\n  public query func getOwner() : async Principal {\n    return nftOwner;\n  };\n\n  public query func getAsset() : async [Nat8] {\n    return imageBytes;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Mint from the CLI and verify</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx identity get-principal\ndfx deploy nft --argument '(&quot;CryptoDunks #123&quot;, principal &quot;&lt;YOUR_PRINCIPAL&gt;&quot;, vec {10; 20; 30})'   # real command carries the full byte list from the README\ndfx canister call nft getName\ndfx canister call nft getOwner\ndfx canister call nft getAsset</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create and mint the NFT canister</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Unzipped opend starter with empty main.mo and prebuilt frontend.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A deployed nft canister, created with an actor class, that returns the name, owner and image bytes you gave it.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create src/NFT/nft.mo and add the nft entry to dfx.json.</li><li>Write a plain actor that prints a message; run npm install, dfx start, dfx deploy and confirm the message.</li><li>Change it to actor class NFT(name, owner, content) and copy parameters into itemName, nftOwner, imageBytes.</li><li>Add query functions getName, getOwner, getAsset.</li><li>Get your principal with dfx identity get-principal.</li><li>Deploy with --argument using the README command, substituting your principal.</li><li>Call getName, getOwner and getAsset with dfx canister call and compare with what you passed.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> getName returns the minted name, getOwner returns your principal, getAsset returns the byte vector.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\n\nactor class NFT(name : Text, owner : Principal, content : [Nat8]) {\n  let itemName = name;\n  let nftOwner = owner;\n  let imageBytes = content;\n\n  public query func getName() : async Text {\n    return itemName;\n  };\n\n  public query func getOwner() : async Principal {\n    return nftOwner;\n  };\n\n  public query func getAsset() : async [Nat8] {\n    return imageBytes;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Wrong path or JSON comma in dfx.json: canister not found or not deployed.</li><li>Deploying an actor class without --argument gives the 'Expected arguments' error.</li><li>Altering the spacing or quotes in the argument string.</li><li>Not replacing the sample principal with your own.</li><li>Typos in query function names; CLI call uses getName without ().</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference main.mo is still the default greet stub in this lesson, so it only matters for the nft canister.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l361", "<p class=\"summary\">Renders the minted NFT in the React Item component: pass the canister id as a prop, create an HttpAgent and an actor from the generated idlFactory, load name, owner and image bytes on mount with useEffect, and turn the byte array into a Blob object URL for the img tag. The owner field is a learner challenge.</p>\n<h3>Starting the frontend</h3>\n<ul><li>Run npm start and open localhost:8080: header, footer and the static home image appear; nothing is clickable yet.</li><li>In App.jsx, comment out the home image (and its import) and render the Item component instead. Letting the editor autocomplete the component name inserts the import automatically, avoiding typos.</li><li>Item.jsx is plain markup: a card with an img, the name heading and an 'Owner:' line. Layout/centering is fixed in later lessons. Initially these are hardcoded values.</li></ul>\n<h3>Passing the canister id as a prop</h3>\n<ul><li>Get the minted canister's id with dfx canister id nft.</li><li>In App, store it in a constant (e.g. nftId) and pass it down as a prop named id: &lt;Item id={nftId} /&gt;. In Item read props.id.</li><li>This id is later used to address the right canister, so each Item can show a different NFT.</li></ul>\n<h3>HttpAgent: talking to the canister over HTTP</h3>\n<ul><li>Calling a canister from the browser means HTTP requests to the replica. Locally that is the local dfx address; define it as a localHost URL string.</li><li>HttpAgent comes from @dfinity/agent: import { HttpAgent } from &quot;@dfinity/agent&quot; (case-sensitive; watch the spelling of dfinity).</li><li>const agent = new HttpAgent({ host: localHost }); the constructor takes an options object where host is the replica URL.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference repo the host constant is a localhost port; use whichever address your dfx or proxy actually serves.</li></ul>\n<h3>Actor.createActor and the idlFactory</h3>\n<ul><li>Calls are asynchronous, so they happen in an async function, here loadNFT().</li><li>Create the actor: await Actor.createActor(idlFactory, { agent, canisterId }). First argument: the interface factory; second: options, including the agent and the target canister id.</li><li>idlFactory is exported from the generated declarations folder, in nft.did.js. It describes the Motoko canister's interface (methods getAsset, getName, getOwner, their inputs and outputs) in JavaScript terms, acting as a translator between frontend and Motoko.</li><li>Import path from the component: three levels up, then declarations/nft, i.e. ../../../declarations/nft. Note exact casing: idlFactory.</li><li>canisterId must be a Principal object, not a string. Import Principal from @dfinity/principal and wrap props.id with Principal.fromText(props.id).</li></ul>\n<h3>When to load: useEffect with an empty dependency array</h3>\n<ul><li>The NFT should be fetched once, on the first render. Use useEffect(() =&gt; { loadNFT(); }, []).</li><li>The second argument is the dependency list; empty means run only after the first render, otherwise the fetch would repeat on every state update.</li></ul>\n<h3>Name, owner and image</h3>\n<ul><li>Name: const name = await NFTActor.getName(); then setName(name) using a state variable created with useState() (initially undefined). Replace the hardcoded heading text with {name}. The demo shows it changing to the minted CryptoDunks name.</li><li>Owner (challenge): await NFTActor.getOwner() returns a Principal; call .toText() before storing it so React can render it, then setOwner. Replace the hardcoded owner text with {owner}.</li><li>Image: getAsset() returns an array of 8-bit numbers. Wrap it in new Uint8Array(imageData) so JavaScript treats it as bytes.</li><li>Create a Blob from it: new Blob([imageContent.buffer], { type: &quot;image/png&quot; }). A Blob takes an array of parts and optional options such as the MIME type (see MDN). .buffer gives the underlying ArrayBuffer.</li><li>URL.createObjectURL(blob) returns a temporary URL string usable as the img src. Store in an image state variable and set src={image}.</li><li>Result: the card shows the minted name, the owner principal and the pixel-art image from the chain for the first time.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Item.jsx data loading (reference-repo version, tidied)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { Actor, HttpAgent } from &quot;@dfinity/agent&quot;;\nimport React, { useEffect, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { idlFactory } from &quot;../../../declarations/nft&quot;;\nimport { Principal } from &quot;@dfinity/principal&quot;;\n\nfunction Item(props) {\n  const [name, setName] = useState();\n  const [owner, setOwner] = useState();\n  const [image, setImage] = useState();\n\n  const id = Principal.fromText(props.id);\n  const localHost = &quot;http://localhost:3000/&quot;;   // use your local replica address\n  const agent = new HttpAgent({ host: localHost });\n\n  async function loadNFT() {\n    const NFTActor = await Actor.createActor(idlFactory, {\n      agent,\n      canisterId: id,\n    });\n    const name = await NFTActor.getName();\n    const owner = await NFTActor.getOwner();\n    const imageData = await NFTActor.getAsset();\n    const imageContent = new Uint8Array(imageData);\n    const blob = new Blob([imageContent.buffer], { type: &quot;image/png&quot; });\n    const image = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\n    setName(name);\n    setOwner(owner.toText());\n    setImage(image);\n  }\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    loadNFT();\n  }, []);\n\n  // JSX: &lt;img src={image} /&gt;, {name}, Owner: {owner}\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx: pass the canister id</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const nftId = &quot;&lt;output of: dfx canister id nft&gt;&quot;;\n// ...\n&lt;Item id={nftId} /&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Show the owner from the NFT canister</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Item.jsx already loads and displays the NFT name from getName() via state, but the owner line is still hardcoded.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Replace the hardcoded owner with the real owner principal of the minted NFT, displayed as text.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add const [owner, setOwner] = useState(); in Item.</li><li>Inside loadNFT call const owner = await NFTActor.getOwner();.</li><li>Convert the Principal with owner.toText() and pass the result to setOwner.</li><li>Replace the hardcoded owner in the JSX with {owner}.</li><li>Save, refresh and compare with the output of dfx canister call nft getOwner.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A long principal id equal to the one used when minting appears after 'Owner:'.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const [owner, setOwner] = useState();\n// in loadNFT:\nconst owner = await NFTActor.getOwner();\nsetOwner(owner.toText());\n// in JSX:\n// &lt;p&gt;Owner: {owner}&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Typos in HttpAgent, @dfinity/agent, or idlFactory casing.</li><li>Passing a string where a Principal is needed (forget Principal.fromText).</li><li>Forgetting await on actor creation or on the method calls.</li><li>Putting a Principal object in JSX instead of toText().</li><li>Omitting the empty array in useEffect, causing repeated fetches.</li><li>Using the wrong dfx address as host.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo has typos (imageContant) and a hardcoded canister id and port; replace them with your own. Its Item.jsx builds the agent on each render, which is fine for the demo.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l362", "<p class=\"summary\">Turns the OpenD minting form into a working feature: the form collects an image and a name with React Hook Form, converts the image file to a byte array, and calls a new mint function on the main canister. That function programmatically creates an NFT canister (funded with cycles) and returns its principal, which the UI then uses to display the new NFT, with a loading spinner during minting.</p>\n<h3>Goal and starting point</h3>\n<ul><li>Previous lesson displayed an NFT that had been minted by hand from the command line. This lesson lets any user upload an image in the browser and have a fresh NFT canister created on the fly.</li><li>Minter.jsx already contains static markup (an upload input, a collection-name input and a Mint NFT button built from a span). The first step is to render it from App.jsx between Header and Footer: comment out the temporary Item and add a self-closing &lt;Minter /&gt; (the editor auto-import adds the import line).</li><li>Data flow overview: user fills inputs -&gt; inputs registered with React Hook Form -&gt; clicking the button calls handleSubmit -&gt; handleSubmit calls onSubmit with the collected data -&gt; onSubmit calls the backend mint function -&gt; backend creates a canister -&gt; its principal returns to the frontend -&gt; Item renders it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Collecting form input with React Hook Form</h3>\n<ul><li>Instead of controlled inputs (value plus onChange), the lesson uses the useForm hook from the react-hook-form package (already installed in the project). Import it: import { useForm } from &quot;react-hook-form&quot;.</li><li>Destructure with curly braces (object destructuring, unlike the array destructuring of useState): const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm();. register is a function used to attach an input to the form; handleSubmit wraps the submit handler.</li><li>Register an input by spreading the result of register into it: &lt;input {...register(&quot;name&quot;, { required: true })} /&gt;. The first argument is the key under which the value will appear in the submitted data object; the options object sets validation, and required: true stops submission when the field is empty.</li><li>Trigger submission from the Mint NFT button: put onClick={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} on the span. handleSubmit validates first and only calls onSubmit when all required fields are filled.</li><li>Define async function onSubmit(data). To verify wiring, console.log(data.name) first; typing text and clicking Mint prints it.</li><li>Do the same for the file input, registering it under the key &quot;image&quot; with required: true. Logging data.image shows a FileList (a file input can hold several files), and the chosen file is element 0. Sample 20x20 CryptoDunks PNGs are provided as a lesson download; they are enlarged by CSS on the frontend.</li><li>Then replace the logs with real values: const name = data.name; const image = data.image[0];.</li></ul>\n<h3>Converting the image to Nat8-compatible bytes</h3>\n<ul><li>The File object carries metadata (file name, size and so on) that should not be stored on the blockchain. Only the raw bytes are needed.</li><li>image.arrayBuffer() (a Blob method) returns a Promise resolving to an ArrayBuffer of the binary content, so it must be awaited.</li><li>Wrap the buffer in new Uint8Array(buffer) to view it as unsigned 8-bit integers, then spread into an array: const imageByteData = [...new Uint8Array(imageArray)];. The resulting plain array of numbers 0-255 is what the Candid type vec nat8 / Motoko [Nat8] expects.</li><li>Splitting it across two lines (const imageArray = await image.arrayBuffer(); then the Uint8Array line) is equivalent and easier to read.</li></ul>\n<h3>Backend: public shared mint function in main.mo</h3>\n<ul><li>Minting lives in the main canister (main.mo), not in the NFT actor class, because main is the canister that creates and tracks NFT canisters (backend-to-backend interaction, later stored in a HashMap).</li><li>Signature: public shared (msg) func mint(imgData : [Nat8], name : Text) : async Principal. shared (msg) exposes the caller's identity via msg.caller. The type must be [Nat8], not [Nat], or it will not be compatible with the NFT class.</li><li>Import Principal (mo:base/Principal) to use the type. Then let owner : Principal = msg.caller;. Once authentication is wired in, the logged-in user's principal becomes the owner. The lesson skips authentication (covered earlier in the course), so the caller is the anonymous principal for now.</li><li>Import the actor class file with a relative path: import NFTActorClass &quot;../NFT/nft&quot;;.</li><li>Create the canister with await NFTActorClass.NFT(name, owner, imgData). The constructor arguments are, in order, the name, the owner and the content. Initialisation is asynchronous, so await is required.</li><li>Returning the new canister's id needs a helper: there is no direct way to read the id from the instance. In nft.mo bind the class instance with = this after the class parameter list and add public query func getCanisterId() : async Principal { return Principal.fromActor(this); }. Principal.fromActor takes an actor and returns its principal; binding this is what lets an actor class (which needs constructor arguments) refer to itself.</li><li>Back in mint: let newNFTPrincipal = await newNFT.getCanisterId(); return newNFTPrincipal;.</li></ul>\n<h3>Cycles for programmatic canister creation</h3>\n<ul><li>On the live Internet Computer every new canister must be funded with cycles. A local dfx replica does not enforce accounting, so it works locally without them, but the code should include them before deployment.</li><li>Use the ExperimentalCycles base module: import Cycles &quot;mo:base/ExperimentalCycles&quot;; then call Cycles.add(amount) immediately before the canister-creating call. The cycles are taken from the main canister's balance and attached to the next outgoing creation call.</li><li>Lecture figures: roughly 100 billion cycles to create a canister, plus about 500 million to keep it running, so add the sum. (Extra) The reference repo simply uses Cycles.add(1_200_000_000_000), a larger round number.</li><li>The module is called experimental because the final cycles API was undecided at the time (2022).</li><li>To observe the effect, import Debug and print Debug.print(debug_show(Cycles.balance())) before and after creating the canister; the second value is lower.</li></ul>\n<h3>Frontend: calling mint and showing the result</h3>\n<ul><li>Import the backend actor into Minter.jsx from the generated declarations folder (relative path with ../../../declarations/OPEND_backend). (Extra) The reference repo imports { OPEND_backend } from that path.</li><li>Inside onSubmit: const newNFTId = await OPEND_backend.mint(imageByteData, name);. The return value is a Principal object. console.log(newNFTId.toText()) shows its textual form; toText is a method of the Principal type from @dfinity/principal.</li><li>Redeploy after backend changes using the deploy command from the project README, then test: both cycle balances print in the replica logs, the second smaller, and the new canister id prints in the browser console.</li><li>Rendering the minted item: Minter keeps state const [nftPrincipal, setNFTPrincipal] = useState(&quot;&quot;). If nftPrincipal is an empty string, return the form; else return a success block (heading 'Minted!' plus a div with class horizontal-center containing &lt;Item id={nftPrincipal.toText()} /&gt;). The markup for the else-branch is supplied in the lesson README to copy rather than type, because of its many class names.</li><li>Item expects a text prop named id, so the Principal must be converted with toText(). After a successful mint, call setNFTPrincipal(newNFTId) to switch views.</li></ul>\n<h3>Loader while minting</h3>\n<ul><li>Minting takes noticeable time, so a spinner (HTML supplied in the README, class lds-ellipsis with four child divs) is placed inside the minter-container above the h3.</li><li>Control it with the hidden attribute: &lt;div hidden={loaderHidden} ...&gt;, state const [loaderHidden, setLoaderHidden] = useState(true). Start hidden; call setLoaderHidden(false) at the start of onSubmit; set it back to true after the canister id returns.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo: mint creates a canister and returns its principal (from reference repo 42.04)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Cycles &quot;mo:base/ExperimentalCycles&quot;;\nimport Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\nimport NFTActorClass &quot;../NFT/nft&quot;;\n\npersistent actor OpenD {\n\n  public shared (msg) func mint(imgData : [Nat8], name : Text) : async Principal {\n    let owner : Principal = msg.caller;\n    Cycles.add(1_200_000_000_000);\n    let newNFT = await NFTActorClass.NFT(name, owner, imgData);\n    let newNFTPrincipal = await newNFT.getCanisterId();\n    return newNFTPrincipal;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">nft.mo: actor class with self-reference for getCanisterId (reference repo, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\n\npersistent actor class NFT(name : Text, owner : Principal, content : [Nat8]) = this {\n  let itemName = name;\n  let nftOwner = owner;\n  let imageBytes = content;\n\n  public query func getName() : async Text { return itemName; };\n  public query func getOwner() : async Principal { return nftOwner; };\n  public query func getAsset() : async [Nat8] { return imageBytes; };\n\n  // &quot;= this&quot; binds the class instance so it can report its own canister id\n  public query func getCanisterId() : async Principal {\n    return Principal.fromActor(this);\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Minter.jsx: form handling, byte conversion, mint call, loader and result view (reference repo, markup trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { useForm } from &quot;react-hook-form&quot;;\nimport { OPEND_backend } from &quot;../../../declarations/OPEND_backend&quot;;\nimport Item from &quot;./Item&quot;;\n\nfunction Minter() {\n  const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm();\n  const [nftPrincipal, setNFTPrincipal] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [loaderHidden, setLoaderHidden] = useState(true);\n\n  async function onSubmit(data) {\n    setLoaderHidden(false);\n    const name = data.name;\n    const image = data.image[0];               // FileList -&gt; first File\n    const imageArray = await image.arrayBuffer();\n    const imageByteData = [...new Uint8Array(imageArray)]; // plain number[] -&gt; [Nat8]\n    const newNFTId = await OPEND_backend.mint(imageByteData, name);\n    setNFTPrincipal(newNFTId);\n    setLoaderHidden(true);\n  }\n\n  if (nftPrincipal == &quot;&quot;) {\n    return (\n      &lt;div className=&quot;minter-container&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;div hidden={loaderHidden} className=&quot;lds-ellipsis&quot;&gt;\n          &lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;h3&gt;Create NFT&lt;/h3&gt;\n        &lt;form&gt;\n          &lt;input {...register(&quot;image&quot;, { required: true })} className=&quot;upload&quot; type=&quot;file&quot; accept=&quot;image/x-png,image/jpeg,image/gif,image/svg+xml,image/webp&quot; /&gt;\n          &lt;input {...register(&quot;name&quot;, { required: true })} placeholder=&quot;e.g. CryptoDunks&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; /&gt;\n          &lt;span onClick={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} className=&quot;form-Chip-label&quot;&gt;Mint NFT&lt;/span&gt;\n        &lt;/form&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    );\n  } else {\n    return (\n      &lt;div className=&quot;minter-container&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;h3&gt;Minted!&lt;/h3&gt;\n        &lt;div className=&quot;horizontal-center&quot;&gt;\n          &lt;Item id={nftPrincipal.toText()} /&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    );\n  }\n}\nexport default Minter;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Enable minting from the Minter form</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> OpenD project from lesson 42.03: nft.mo actor class (name, owner, content), main.mo backend, Item component that renders an NFT by canister id, and a static Minter.jsx not yet used in App.jsx.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A user selects an image, types a name, clicks Mint NFT, sees a loader, and then sees the newly minted NFT (image, name, owner) rendered by Item.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In App.jsx, comment out the hard-coded Item and render &lt;Minter /&gt; between Header and Footer.</li><li>In Minter.jsx call useForm, destructure register and handleSubmit, and register the file input as &quot;image&quot; and the text input as &quot;name&quot;, both with required: true.</li><li>Add onClick={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} to the Mint NFT span and define async function onSubmit(data); log data.name and data.image to confirm wiring (image is a FileList).</li><li>Take data.image[0], await arrayBuffer(), and spread new Uint8Array(buffer) into an array of byte numbers.</li><li>In nft.mo, bind the class with = this and add getCanisterId using Principal.fromActor(this).</li><li>In main.mo import Cycles, Principal and the NFT class; add public shared (msg) func mint(imgData : [Nat8], name : Text) : async Principal that sets owner to msg.caller, calls Cycles.add, awaits NFTActorClass.NFT(name, owner, imgData), then returns await newNFT.getCanisterId().</li><li>Import the backend actor in Minter.jsx and call await mint(imageByteData, name) inside onSubmit; store the result via setNFTPrincipal.</li><li>Add the if/else render: form when nftPrincipal is an empty string, otherwise the 'Minted!' block with &lt;Item id={nftPrincipal.toText()} /&gt;.</li><li>Paste the loader markup above the h3, bind hidden to loaderHidden state (initially true), set false on submit and true when mint finishes.</li><li>Redeploy with the README command and test with a CryptoDunk image.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> After clicking Mint NFT the spinner appears; when mint returns, the form is replaced by a 'Minted!' heading and a card showing the uploaded image, its name and the owner principal. The replica log shows the main canister's cycle balance dropping after creation.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { useForm } from &quot;react-hook-form&quot;;\nimport { OPEND_backend } from &quot;../../../declarations/OPEND_backend&quot;;\nimport Item from &quot;./Item&quot;;\n\nfunction Minter() {\n  const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm();\n  const [nftPrincipal, setNFTPrincipal] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [loaderHidden, setLoaderHidden] = useState(true);\n\n  async function onSubmit(data) {\n    setLoaderHidden(false);\n    const name = data.name;\n    const image = data.image[0];               // FileList -&gt; first File\n    const imageArray = await image.arrayBuffer();\n    const imageByteData = [...new Uint8Array(imageArray)]; // plain number[] -&gt; [Nat8]\n    const newNFTId = await OPEND_backend.mint(imageByteData, name);\n    setNFTPrincipal(newNFTId);\n    setLoaderHidden(true);\n  }\n\n  if (nftPrincipal == &quot;&quot;) {\n    return (\n      &lt;div className=&quot;minter-container&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;div hidden={loaderHidden} className=&quot;lds-ellipsis&quot;&gt;\n          &lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;h3&gt;Create NFT&lt;/h3&gt;\n        &lt;form&gt;\n          &lt;input {...register(&quot;image&quot;, { required: true })} className=&quot;upload&quot; type=&quot;file&quot; accept=&quot;image/x-png,image/jpeg,image/gif,image/svg+xml,image/webp&quot; /&gt;\n          &lt;input {...register(&quot;name&quot;, { required: true })} placeholder=&quot;e.g. CryptoDunks&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; /&gt;\n          &lt;span onClick={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} className=&quot;form-Chip-label&quot;&gt;Mint NFT&lt;/span&gt;\n        &lt;/form&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    );\n  } else {\n    return (\n      &lt;div className=&quot;minter-container&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;h3&gt;Minted!&lt;/h3&gt;\n        &lt;div className=&quot;horizontal-center&quot;&gt;\n          &lt;Item id={nftPrincipal.toText()} /&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    );\n  }\n}\nexport default Minter;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using [Nat] instead of [Nat8] for the image data in mint makes it incompatible with the NFT class.</li><li>Passing the Principal object to Item instead of a text string; use toText() so Principal.fromText in Item works.</li><li>Forgetting the return in one branch of the if/else gives the React error that nothing was returned from render; both branches need a return.</li><li>Not awaiting arrayBuffer() or the canister constructor.</li><li>Leaving out Cycles.add works locally but fails on the live network.</li><li>Uploading the raw File or FileList instead of its bytes; metadata is not wanted on-chain.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference Minter.jsx imports unused { set } from react-hook-form and Principal; these can be dropped.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l363", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds client-side navigation to OpenD with react-router-dom (Home, Discover, Minter, My NFTs) and gives the backend memory of what was minted: two HashMaps record every NFT and each owner's list of NFT ids. A new getOwnedNFTs query returns the ids, and the My NFTs route maps them into a Gallery of Item components.</p>\n<h3>Why routing is needed</h3>\n<ul><li>After minting, the user needs a place to see everything they own. The nav buttons (Discover, Minter, My NFTs) are inactive, so react-router-dom (a dependency already listed in package.json, like react-hook-form) is used to switch views by URL path.</li><li>Preparation in App.jsx: comment out the temporary Minter and the hard-coded NFT id, so that only Header and Footer remain; the Header will own the routing and page content.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The code uses react-router-dom v5 (^5.2.0 in the reference package.json), where Switch exists. In v6 Switch was renamed Routes and routes take an element prop, so the syntax below does not carry over directly.</li></ul>\n<h3>BrowserRouter and Link</h3>\n<ul><li>Import { BrowserRouter, Link, Switch, Route } from &quot;react-router-dom&quot; in Header.jsx.</li><li>Wrap everything the Header returns in &lt;BrowserRouter&gt;. It provides the routing context that Link and Route require.</li><li>Link turns text into a navigation link without a full page request. Its to attribute is the path: /discover, /minter, /collection (the My NFTs collection). Wrap the link around the text inside each button, not around the button itself, and use a different path on each of the three.</li><li>Also wrap the OpenD title (the h5) in &lt;Link to=&quot;/&quot;&gt; so clicking the logo text returns to the home route.</li></ul>\n<h3>Switch and Route</h3>\n<ul><li>Switch inspects the current URL and renders the first Route whose path matches, much like a JavaScript switch statement checking cases from top to bottom. Put it beneath the header markup, still inside BrowserRouter.</li><li>Four Route children are needed: home, discover, minter, collection. Each has a path attribute that must match the corresponding Link's to value exactly (copy-paste to avoid typos), and whatever sits between the Route tags is what gets rendered.</li><li>Home route (path &quot;/&quot;): the home image, moved out of App.jsx together with its import line (the image import now lives in Header.jsx).</li><li>Discover route: only a placeholder &lt;h1&gt;Discover&lt;/h1&gt; for now; it will list NFTs for sale once listing is built in later lessons.</li><li>Minter route: &lt;Minter /&gt;, imported from ./Minter.</li><li>Collection route: the Gallery of the user's NFTs (first with a hard-coded Item, then dynamic data, see below).</li><li>Matching problem: the path &quot;/&quot; matches every URL, so if it comes first it always wins. Two fixes: move it to the end of the Switch, or (preferred) add the exact attribute so it matches only when the path is exactly &quot;/&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h3>Making Gallery reusable</h3>\n<ul><li>Gallery was a static grid with one Item. Item breaks without an id (Principal.fromText on undefined), which showed up as a blank My NFTs page; a temporary fix was to give the Item the canister id used earlier.</li><li>The heading was hard-coded as Discover. Replace it with {props.title} and pass title=&quot;My NFTs&quot; from where Gallery is used, so the same component can serve Discover and My NFTs later.</li></ul>\n<h3>Backend: tracking NFTs and owners</h3>\n<ul><li>Until now mint only returned the new canister id and nothing was remembered. The backend needs a data store in main.mo, built with HashMap (import HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;).</li><li>mapOfNFTs: a var HashMap whose key is the NFT canister's Principal and whose value is the NFTActorClass.NFT instance. Constructed as HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, NFTActorClass.NFT&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash): the first argument is an initial capacity, then the key-equality function and the key-hash function. After obtaining newNFTPrincipal in mint, call mapOfNFTs.put(newNFTPrincipal, newNFT).</li><li>mapOfOwners: key is the owner's Principal, value is a List.List&lt;Principal&gt; of the NFT canister ids that owner holds, because one owner can have many NFTs. Import List (mo:base/List). Same constructor arguments (Principal keys).</li><li>A plain put is not enough for mapOfOwners since the existing list must be read, extended and written back. This is done in a separate private func addToOwnershipMap(owner : Principal, nftId : Principal). private keeps it unreachable from outside the actor.</li><li>Inside it: mapOfOwners.get(owner) returns an option (?List), because a first-time owner has no entry. Resolve it with a switch expression wrapped in parentheses: case null returns an empty list via List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;(); case (?result) returns the unwrapped result. Declaring the variable's type as List.List&lt;Principal&gt; is what makes the compiler complain until the option is unwrapped.</li><li>Then ownedNFTs := List.push(nftId, ownedNFTs); (push puts the new element at the head and returns the new list, so the variable must be reassigned with :=), followed by mapOfOwners.put(owner, ownedNFTs).</li><li>Call addToOwnershipMap(owner, newNFTPrincipal) from mint after the mapOfNFTs.put.</li><li>The lecture mentions a naming typo in the earlier return (variable name mismatch) that happened not to cause an error; keep newNFTPrincipal consistent.</li></ul>\n<h3>Backend: query for a user's NFTs (exercise)</h3>\n<ul><li>Add public query func getOwnedNFTs(user : Principal) : async [Principal]. A pause-and-try moment: repeat the same get-and-unwrap switch used above.</li><li>Declare var userNFTs : List.List&lt;Principal&gt; = switch (mapOfOwners.get(user)) { case null List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;(); case (?result) result }; then return List.toArray(userNFTs) to convert the list into an array, the type sent to the frontend as a JS array of Principals.</li></ul>\n<h3>Frontend: fetching and rendering the owned NFTs</h3>\n<ul><li>In Header.jsx import the backend actor from ../../../declarations/OPEND_backend and write async function getNFTs() that does const userNFTIds = await OPEND_backend.getOwnedNFTs(CURRENT_USER_ID);. Log it first to check the array.</li><li>CURRENT_USER_ID is the anonymous principal (&quot;2vxsx-fae&quot;) exported from main.jsx, used so the login flow can be skipped. Import it into Header with the default import from ../main. Both lines defining and exporting it must exist in main.jsx.</li><li>Run getNFTs once on first render with useEffect(() =&gt; { getNFTs(); }, []); the empty dependency array limits it to a single call.</li><li>Redeploy using the README command; after minting a few more NFTs the logged array contains one principal per mint (the lecture's test user had three).</li><li>Store a ready-made Gallery element in state: const [userOwnedGallery, setOwnedGallery] = useState(); then in getNFTs call setOwnedGallery(&lt;Gallery title=&quot;My NFTs&quot; ids={userNFTIds} /&gt;). The collection Route renders {userOwnedGallery}.</li><li>Gallery: const [items, setItems] = useState(); a function fetchNFTs that checks props.ids != undefined and then calls setItems(props.ids.map(NFTId =&gt; &lt;Item id={NFTId} key={NFTId.toText()} /&gt;)). The arrow function returns JSX wrapped in parentheses (not braces) for an implicit return.</li><li>React requires a unique key on list-rendered elements; the principal converted with toText() is a convenient unique value (Principal import needed only if used for typing).</li><li>Call fetchNFTs inside useEffect with an empty dependency array, and render {items} inside the innermost grid div in the Gallery markup. Items placed outside that div display stacked on separate lines; check the closing div position if the layout looks off.</li><li>Item.jsx change: ids from the gallery are Principal objects, not strings, so Item should stop calling Principal.fromText and just use props.id directly. (Extra) In the reference repo Minter still passes nftPrincipal.toText(), a string; the agent's createActor accepts either a string or a Principal for canisterId, so both paths work.</li><li>Refresh problem: a freshly minted NFT only appears in My NFTs after a manual page reload, because Header (and its useEffect) ran before the mint. Setting forceRefresh={true} on BrowserRouter makes each Link navigation do a full page reload, re-running the effect so the new NFT shows. (Extra) This is a blunt fix; re-fetching on route change or lifting state up would avoid full reloads.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo: HashMaps, private ownership helper, and getOwnedNFTs (reference repo 42.05)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Cycles &quot;mo:base/ExperimentalCycles&quot;;\nimport Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\nimport HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;;\nimport List &quot;mo:base/List&quot;;\nimport NFTActorClass &quot;../NFT/nft&quot;;\n\npersistent actor OpenD {\n\n  // NFT canister id -&gt; the NFT actor itself\n  transient var mapOfNFTs = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, NFTActorClass.NFT&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n  // owner principal -&gt; list of NFT canister ids they own\n  transient var mapOfOwners = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, List.List&lt;Principal&gt;&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\n  public shared (msg) func mint(imgData : [Nat8], name : Text) : async Principal {\n    let owner : Principal = msg.caller;\n    Cycles.add(10_000_000_000_000);\n    let newNFT = await NFTActorClass.NFT(name, owner, imgData);\n    let newNFTPrincipal = await newNFT.getCanisterId();\n\n    mapOfNFTs.put(newNFTPrincipal, newNFT);\n    addToOwnershipMap(owner, newNFTPrincipal);\n\n    return newNFTPrincipal;\n  };\n\n  private func addToOwnershipMap(owner : Principal, nftId : Principal) {\n    var ownedNFTs : List.List&lt;Principal&gt; = switch (mapOfOwners.get(owner)) {\n      case null List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;();\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    ownedNFTs := List.push(nftId, ownedNFTs);\n    mapOfOwners.put(owner, ownedNFTs);\n  };\n\n  public query func getOwnedNFTs(user : Principal) : async [Principal] {\n    var userNFTs : List.List&lt;Principal&gt; = switch (mapOfOwners.get(user)) {\n      case null List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;();\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    return List.toArray(userNFTs);\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Header.jsx: BrowserRouter, Links, Switch/Route and owned-NFT fetching (reference repo, class strings abbreviated)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useEffect, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport logo from &quot;/logo.png&quot;;\nimport { BrowserRouter, Link, Switch, Route } from &quot;react-router-dom&quot;;\nimport homeImage from &quot;/home-img.png&quot;;\nimport Minter from &quot;./Minter&quot;;\nimport Gallery from &quot;./Gallery&quot;;\nimport { OPEND_backend } from &quot;../../../declarations/OPEND_backend&quot;;\nimport CURRENT_USER_ID from &quot;../main&quot;;\n\nfunction Header() {\n  const [userOwnedGallery, setOwnedGallery] = useState();\n\n  async function getNFTs() {\n    const userNFTIds = await OPEND_backend.getOwnedNFTs(CURRENT_USER_ID);\n    setOwnedGallery(&lt;Gallery title=&quot;My NFTs&quot; ids={userNFTIds} /&gt;);\n  }\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; { getNFTs(); }, []);\n\n  return (\n    &lt;BrowserRouter forceRefresh={true}&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;app-root-1&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;header className=&quot;...&quot;&gt;\n          &lt;div className=&quot;...&quot;&gt;\n            &lt;img className=&quot;header-logo-11&quot; src={logo} /&gt;\n            &lt;Link to=&quot;/&quot;&gt;&lt;h5 className=&quot;header-logo-text&quot;&gt;OpenD&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;/Link&gt;\n            &lt;button className=&quot;...&quot;&gt;&lt;Link to=&quot;/discover&quot;&gt;Discover&lt;/Link&gt;&lt;/button&gt;\n            &lt;button className=&quot;...&quot;&gt;&lt;Link to=&quot;/minter&quot;&gt;Minter&lt;/Link&gt;&lt;/button&gt;\n            &lt;button className=&quot;...&quot;&gt;&lt;Link to=&quot;/collection&quot;&gt;My NFTs&lt;/Link&gt;&lt;/button&gt;\n          &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;/header&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;Switch&gt;\n        &lt;Route exact path=&quot;/&quot;&gt;&lt;img className=&quot;bottom-space&quot; src={homeImage} /&gt;&lt;/Route&gt;\n        &lt;Route path=&quot;/discover&quot;&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Discover&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/Route&gt;\n        &lt;Route path=&quot;/minter&quot;&gt;&lt;Minter /&gt;&lt;/Route&gt;\n        &lt;Route path=&quot;/collection&quot;&gt;{userOwnedGallery}&lt;/Route&gt;\n      &lt;/Switch&gt;\n    &lt;/BrowserRouter&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Header;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Gallery.jsx: render one Item per id (reference repo, typo itemms fixed)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useEffect, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Item from &quot;./Item&quot;;\n\nfunction Gallery(props) {\n  const [items, setItems] = useState();\n\n  function fetchNFTs() {\n    if (props.ids != undefined) {\n      setItems(\n        props.ids.map((NFTId) =&gt; &lt;Item id={NFTId} key={NFTId.toText()} /&gt;)\n      );\n    }\n  }\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; { fetchNFTs(); }, []);\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;gallery-view&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h3 className=&quot;makeStyles-title-99 Typography-h3&quot;&gt;{props.title}&lt;/h3&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;disGrid-root disGrid-container disGrid-spacing-xs-2&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;div className=&quot;disGrid-root disGrid-item disGrid-grid-xs-12&quot;&gt;\n          &lt;div className=&quot;disGrid-root disGrid-container disGrid-spacing-xs-5 disGrid-justify-content-xs-center&quot;&gt;\n            {items}\n          &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Gallery;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx after the change: routing lives in Header</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Header from &quot;./components/Header&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./components/Footer&quot;;\nimport &quot;bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;App&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;   {/* Header now also holds the router and the page content */}\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Routing plus 'My NFTs' gallery</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project from 42.04: working Minter, static Gallery with a single Item, inactive nav buttons, main.mo with only a mint function.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clicking the nav items switches between Home, Discover, Minter and My NFTs; My NFTs lists an Item card for every NFT minted by the current (anonymous) user, including newly minted ones.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Comment out Minter and the hard-coded nftId in App.jsx; keep Header and Footer.</li><li>In Header.jsx import BrowserRouter, Link, Switch, Route; wrap the returned markup in BrowserRouter.</li><li>Wrap the text of each nav button in a Link with to=&quot;/discover&quot;, &quot;/minter&quot;, &quot;/collection&quot;, and wrap the OpenD title in a Link to &quot;/&quot;.</li><li>Add a Switch with four Routes: exact path &quot;/&quot; (home image), &quot;/discover&quot; (h1 placeholder), &quot;/minter&quot; (Minter), &quot;/collection&quot; (Gallery). Move the home image and its import from App into the Route.</li><li>Make Gallery accept props.title and pass title=&quot;My NFTs&quot;.</li><li>In main.mo add mapOfNFTs and mapOfOwners HashMaps, populate mapOfNFTs in mint, and write private addToOwnershipMap with the option-unwrapping switch and List.push; call it from mint.</li><li>Write public query func getOwnedNFTs(user : Principal) : async [Principal] returning List.toArray of the user's list (empty list when the user is unknown).</li><li>In Header.jsx call getOwnedNFTs(CURRENT_USER_ID) inside an async function triggered by useEffect with an empty array; store &lt;Gallery title=&quot;My NFTs&quot; ids={...} /&gt; in useState and render it in the collection Route.</li><li>In Gallery.jsx map props.ids to &lt;Item id={...} key={...toText()} /&gt; via state and useEffect and render the result inside the grid container.</li><li>Update Item.jsx to use props.id directly (a Principal) instead of parsing text.</li><li>Redeploy, mint several NFTs, open My NFTs; add forceRefresh={true} to BrowserRouter so new NFTs appear without a manual reload.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Header links change the displayed page. Home shows the landing image, Discover shows a heading, Minter shows the minting form, My NFTs shows a grid of cards (image, name, owner) for every NFT the anonymous user has minted, with the newest first because List.push adds at the head.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Cycles &quot;mo:base/ExperimentalCycles&quot;;\nimport Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\nimport HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;;\nimport List &quot;mo:base/List&quot;;\nimport NFTActorClass &quot;../NFT/nft&quot;;\n\npersistent actor OpenD {\n\n  // NFT canister id -&gt; the NFT actor itself\n  transient var mapOfNFTs = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, NFTActorClass.NFT&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n  // owner principal -&gt; list of NFT canister ids they own\n  transient var mapOfOwners = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, List.List&lt;Principal&gt;&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\n  public shared (msg) func mint(imgData : [Nat8], name : Text) : async Principal {\n    let owner : Principal = msg.caller;\n    Cycles.add(10_000_000_000_000);\n    let newNFT = await NFTActorClass.NFT(name, owner, imgData);\n    let newNFTPrincipal = await newNFT.getCanisterId();\n\n    mapOfNFTs.put(newNFTPrincipal, newNFT);\n    addToOwnershipMap(owner, newNFTPrincipal);\n\n    return newNFTPrincipal;\n  };\n\n  private func addToOwnershipMap(owner : Principal, nftId : Principal) {\n    var ownedNFTs : List.List&lt;Principal&gt; = switch (mapOfOwners.get(owner)) {\n      case null List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;();\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    ownedNFTs := List.push(nftId, ownedNFTs);\n    mapOfOwners.put(owner, ownedNFTs);\n  };\n\n  public query func getOwnedNFTs(user : Principal) : async [Principal] {\n    var userNFTs : List.List&lt;Principal&gt; = switch (mapOfOwners.get(user)) {\n      case null List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;();\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    return List.toArray(userNFTs);\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting the Link around the button instead of the text, or giving two buttons the same path.</li><li>Link to values not matching Route paths exactly, for example a typo or capitalisation difference.</li><li>Leaving the root Route without exact (or not placing it last), so it matches every URL.</li><li>Calling Gallery's Item without an id, which crashes rendering and leaves My NFTs blank.</li><li>Forgetting that mapOfOwners.get returns an option; assigning it straight to a List type is a compile error.</li><li>Forgetting to reassign after List.push (it returns a new list) and to put the result back into the map.</li><li>Keeping Principal.fromText in Item while passing Principal objects from the Gallery.</li><li>Using curly braces instead of parentheses in the map callback, so no JSX is returned.</li><li>Placing {items} outside the grid container div, which breaks the layout.</li><li>New mints not appearing until a manual reload unless forceRefresh is set.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference Gallery calls the state variable itemms (typo); other leftovers such as unused imports in the reference code can be removed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> HashMaps declared as transient var in the reference repo (a persistent actor) are cleared on every canister upgrade; deployments that reinstall the code lose the ownership data.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l364", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds a Sell flow to each owned NFT card: a reusable Button component, a price input, a new Motoko listItem function with a Listing record and HashMap, and a transferOwnership function that hands the NFT to the OpenD canister. Includes debugging of a Nat conversion error and a certificate verification error on the local replica.</p>\n<h3>Overall flow being built</h3>\n<ul><li>Each NFT card under My NFTs gets a Sell button. Clicking it reveals a price input and turns the button into Confirm.</li><li>Confirm calls the backend to record the listing (owner + price) in a map, then moves ownership of the NFT canister to the OpenD marketplace canister.</li><li>Ownership is moved at listing time because only the current owner can call the NFT's transfer function; the marketplace cannot wait around for a buyer and then ask the seller to transfer. OpenD can later pass the NFT to the buyer.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reusable Button component (Button.jsx)</h3>\n<ul><li>Create components/Button.jsx: a functional component Button that returns the button markup supplied in the project README (copy only the content between the outer div tags), wrapped in return ( ... ), then export default Button.</li><li>It needs import React from &quot;react&quot;; the useEffect/useState imports are not needed here.</li><li>The span's onClick is wired to props.handleClick, so the parent decides what a click does (a callback passed as a prop).</li><li>The label is hard-coded at first, then replaced with {props.text} so the same component can read Sell and later Confirm.</li></ul>\n<h3>Item.jsx state and handlers</h3>\n<ul><li>New state: button (the JSX for the current button) and priceInput (JSX for the input, empty until Sell is clicked). Both are rendered inside the card content, with priceInput above button, after the owner paragraph.</li><li>At the end of loadNFT, setButton(&lt;Button handleClick={handleSell} text={&quot;Sell&quot;} /&gt;) puts the Sell button on the card.</li><li>handleSell first just logs 'Sell clicked' to prove the callback prop works, then calls setPriceInput with a number input (placeholder 'Price in DANG', className price-input; markup is in the README) and swaps the button via setButton to a Button with text Confirm and handleClick={sellItem}.</li><li>The price is held in a plain let variable (let price;) declared in the component, and the input's onChange does price = e.target.value. A state hook is avoided here because the later async calls cannot rely on state having updated by the time sellItem reads it.</li><li>sellItem is an async function because it talks to the backend; first it only logs the price to confirm the variable is reachable.</li></ul>\n<h3>Motoko: Listing type and listings map</h3>\n<ul><li>Define a custom record type with the type keyword: private type Listing = { itemOwner : Principal; itemPrice : Nat; }; Fields are separated by semicolons. Type names start with a capital letter.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The record could later carry sale history or timestamps; it is kept to two fields for simplicity.</li><li>Add a third map next to mapOfNFTs and mapOfOwners: a HashMap keyed by the NFT's Principal with Listing values, created with initial capacity 1, Principal.equal and Principal.hash. The lecture calls it mapOfListings; the reference repo spells it mapOfListing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Motoko: listItem(id, price)</h3>\n<ul><li>Declared as public shared (msg) func so the caller's identity is available as msg.caller. Takes id : Principal and price : Nat and returns async Text so the frontend gets a readable status.</li><li>Look up the NFT with a switch on mapOfNFTs.get(id). The result is an optional, so handle case null (return a 'does not exist' text, which exits the function early) and case (?result) result.</li><li>Fetch the real owner with await item.getOwner(). Forgetting await produces a type error complaining that an async Principal cannot be used as a Principal.</li><li>Authorisation check: if Principal.equal(owner, msg.caller) the caller owns the NFT; otherwise return a 'you don't own the NFT' message.</li><li>On success build a Listing (itemOwner = owner, itemPrice = price), call mapOfListings.put(id, newListing) and return &quot;Success&quot;. The success return belongs inside the if branch so it is only returned when a listing was made.</li></ul>\n<h3>Frontend call and the Nat bug</h3>\n<ul><li>Import the backend: import { OPEND_backend } from &quot;../../../declarations/OPEND_backend&quot; (the lecture refers to it as opend, the reference repo uses the OPEND_backend export).</li><li>In sellItem: const listingResult = await OPEND_backend.listItem(props.id, Number(price)).</li><li>The backend was changed, so redeploy all canisters (command in the README) before testing; this resets state, so mint a fresh NFT first.</li><li>Error 'Invalid Nat argument: &quot;12&quot;' means the price reached Candid as a string (e.target.value is always a string). Wrapping it in Number() fixes it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Motoko: transferOwnership in nft.mo</h3>\n<ul><li>Another public shared (msg) func, taking the new owner's Principal and returning async Text.</li><li>Guard: only proceed if msg.caller equals the stored nftOwner; otherwise return an error text saying the call was not initiated by the owner.</li><li>To change the owner, nftOwner must be declared var (not let), and the update uses the := assignment operator, then returns &quot;Success&quot;.</li><li>Make the NFT's fields private so only code inside the actor, and for the owner only this function, can change them.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo also adds a Bool parameter isListing and a listedForSale flag in the NFT actor; this is groundwork for later lectures and is not in this lecture's walkthrough.</li></ul>\n<h3>Giving the frontend OpenD's own ID</h3>\n<ul><li>Add public query func getOpenDCanisterID() : async Principal { return Principal.fromActor(OpenD); } to the main actor. Principal.fromActor takes the actor name and returns its canister ID.</li><li>In sellItem, once listingResult is the success text, fetch it with await OPEND_backend.getOpenDCanisterID() and pass it as the new owner to NFTActor.transferOwnership(...), then log the returned text.</li><li>To make the NFT actor reachable from sellItem, declare let NFTActor; at component level and change the assignment inside loadNFT from const to plain assignment so both functions share it.</li><li>Status strings are plain text only for readability; error codes or variants would work too.</li></ul>\n<h3>Local replica certificate error</h3>\n<ul><li>After the transfer was added the console showed 'Fail to verify certificate'. By default HttpAgent assumes the live Internet Computer and checks responses with a built-in root public key, which does not match a local replica.</li><li>Fix: call agent.fetchRootKey() right after creating the HttpAgent when running locally.</li><li>This line must be removed before deploying to the real network (leave a TODO comment).</li><li>Testing locally also avoids paying cycles for each minted NFT canister.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying the result</h3>\n<ul><li>Mint a new NFT, open My NFTs, click Sell, enter a price, click Confirm. The console should show 'listing: Success' then 'transfer: Success'.</li><li>Redeploy after each backend edit (both nft.mo and main.mo saved first).</li><li>Next lecture tidies the UI and shows listed items on the Discover page.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Button.jsx (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nexport default function Button(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;Chip-root makeStyles-chipBlue-108 Chip-clickable&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;span onClick={props.handleClick} className=&quot;form-Chip-label&quot;&gt;\n        {props.text}\n      &lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Item.jsx: sell flow handlers</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">let price;\nfunction handleSell() {\n  setPriceInput(\n    &lt;input\n      placeholder=&quot;Price in DANG&quot;\n      type=&quot;number&quot;\n      className=&quot;price-input&quot;\n      value={price}\n      onChange={(e) =&gt; (price = e.target.value)}\n    /&gt;\n  );\n  setButton(&lt;Button handleClick={sellItem} text={&quot;Confirm&quot;} /&gt;);\n}\n\nasync function sellItem() {\n  const listingResult = await OPEND_backend.listItem(props.id, Number(price));\n  console.log(&quot;listing: &quot; + listingResult);\n  if (listingResult == &quot;Success&quot;) {\n    const openDId = await OPEND_backend.getOpenDCanisterID();\n    const transferResult = await NFTActor.transferOwnership(openDId, true);\n    console.log(&quot;transfer: &quot; + transferResult);\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo: Listing type, map and listItem</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">private type Listing = {\n  itemOwner : Principal;\n  itemPrice : Nat;\n};\n\ntransient var mapOfListing = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Listing&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\npublic shared (msg) func listItem(id : Principal, price : Nat) : async Text {\n  var item : NFTActorClass.NFT = switch (mapOfNFTs.get(id)) {\n    case null return &quot;NFT does not exist.&quot;;\n    case (?result) result;\n  };\n\n  let owner = await item.getOwner();\n\n  if (Principal.equal(owner, msg.caller)) {\n    let newListing : Listing = { itemOwner = owner; itemPrice = price };\n    mapOfListing.put(id, newListing);\n    return &quot;Success&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;You don't own the NFT.&quot;;\n  };\n};\n\npublic query func getOpenDCanisterID() : async Principal {\n  return Principal.fromActor(OpenD);\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">nft.mo: owner-only transfer (lecture version, without the isListing flag)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">private var nftOwner = owner;\n\npublic shared (msg) func transferOwnership(newOwner : Principal) : async Text {\n  if (msg.caller == nftOwner) {\n    nftOwner := newOwner;\n    return &quot;Success&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Error: Not initiated by the owner of the NFT.&quot;;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Local-only agent fix</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const agent = new HttpAgent({ host: localHost });\n// TODO: remove when deploying to the live network\nagent.fetchRootKey();</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add a Sell button that lists an NFT and transfers it to OpenD</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project from the previous lesson: Item.jsx shows name, owner and image of each owned NFT; main.mo has mapOfNFTs, mapOfOwners, mint and getOwnedNFTs; nft.mo has getters only. README holds the Button and price-input markup and the deploy commands.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clicking Sell on an owned NFT shows a price input and Confirm button; Confirm records a listing in the backend and moves the NFT's ownership to the OpenD canister, logging two Success messages.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create components/Button.jsx using README markup, props.handleClick on onClick and props.text as the label.</li><li>In Item.jsx add button and priceInput state, render both in the card, and set the Sell button at the end of loadNFT with handleSell as handleClick.</li><li>Write handleSell: log, set the price input JSX (onChange writes e.target.value into a let price), and switch the button to Confirm with sellItem.</li><li>In main.mo add the Listing type, the listings HashMap and listItem with the not-found and owner checks.</li><li>Import OPEND_backend in Item.jsx and call listItem(props.id, Number(price)) inside sellItem; redeploy, mint a fresh NFT and test.</li><li>Add transferOwnership to nft.mo (owner check, var nftOwner, := assignment) and make the fields private.</li><li>Add getOpenDCanisterID to main.mo; in sellItem fetch it and call NFTActor.transferOwnership after a successful listing (hoist NFTActor to a let).</li><li>Add agent.fetchRootKey() after creating the agent, redeploy, mint again and confirm 'listing: Success' and 'transfer: Success'.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Console shows listing: Success and transfer: Success; the NFT is in the listings map and owned by the OpenD canister.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func listItem(id : Principal, price : Nat) : async Text {\n  var item : NFTActorClass.NFT = switch (mapOfNFTs.get(id)) {\n    case null return &quot;NFT does not exist.&quot;;\n    case (?result) result;\n  };\n  let owner = await item.getOwner();\n  if (Principal.equal(owner, msg.caller)) {\n    mapOfListing.put(id, { itemOwner = owner; itemPrice = price });\n    return &quot;Success&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;You don't own the NFT.&quot;;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Passing the price as a string to a Nat parameter gives 'Invalid Nat argument'; convert with Number().</li><li>Forgetting await on item.getOwner() gives an async Principal type mismatch.</li><li>Missing agent.fetchRootKey() on a local replica gives 'Fail to verify certificate'; remove it for live deployment.</li><li>Redeploying resets local state, so previously minted NFTs vanish and must be re-minted.</li><li>Leaving nftOwner as a let prevents reassignment; it must be var and updated with :=.</li><li>Leaving NFT fields public lets other actors alter them; keep them private.</li><li>The success return must sit inside the owner-check branch only.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l365", "<p class=\"summary\">Gives visual feedback to the listing flow in Item.jsx: a loader during listing, hiding the sell controls afterwards, showing OpenD as owner, blurring the image and tagging the card as Listed. Adds an isListed query to the backend so the listed look survives a page refresh.</p>\n<h3>Loader while listing</h3>\n<ul><li>Copy the loader markup (the lds-ellipsis div with four empty child divs) from the README or from Minter.jsx and place it right after the img in Item.jsx.</li><li>Add state const [loaderHidden, setLoaderHidden] = useState(true) and bind it to the loader's hidden attribute, so it is invisible initially.</li><li>In sellItem call setLoaderHidden(false) at the start. After the transfer result equals &quot;Success&quot;, call setLoaderHidden(true).</li></ul>\n<h3>Cleaning up the card after the sale is listed</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside the success branch call setButton() and setPriceInput() with no argument. This resets both to undefined, so React renders nothing for them.</li><li>Replace the owner text with the literal &quot;OpenD&quot; using setOwner, because the real owner value is now the marketplace canister's Principal, which tells the user nothing.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Items already transferred to OpenD cannot be sold again by the user since they no longer own them; the lecture defers handling this.</li></ul>\n<h3>Blur effect with inline style</h3>\n<ul><li>State blur starts undefined and is passed to the image as style={blur}. React inline styles take an object.</li><li>At the start of sellItem call setBlur({ filter: &quot;blur(4px)&quot; }). The CSS filter property with blur(4px) softens the image while keeping it recognisable.</li><li>The blur is deliberately never removed after success, signalling that the NFT is listed and no longer interactive.</li></ul>\n<h3>Persisting the listed look: isListed query</h3>\n<ul><li>On refresh the component re-runs loadNFT, which knows nothing about the listing, so the backend must be asked.</li><li>Add public query func isListed(id : Principal) : async Bool to main.mo. It checks whether mapOfListings.get(id) equals null: null gives false, otherwise true.</li><li>In loadNFT, before setting the button, call const nftIsListed = await OPEND_backend.isListed(props.id). If true: setOwner(&quot;OpenD&quot;) and setBlur(...). If false: setButton with the Sell button. Since button state starts empty, listed items never get a Sell button.</li><li>After editing the backend, redeploy all canisters and check that the generated .did interface contains isListed. Minted NFTs vanish on redeploy.</li></ul>\n<h3>Listed label</h3>\n<ul><li>A span with class purple-text sits inside the name heading. Add state sellStatus initialised to an empty string and render it as {sellStatus} inside the span; start the span content with a space so it separates from the name.</li><li>Set sellStatus to &quot;Listed&quot; both at the end of a successful sale and in loadNFT when nftIsListed is true.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo: isListed</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public query func isListed(id : Principal) : async Bool {\n  if (mapOfListings.get(id) == null) {\n    return false;\n  } else {\n    return true;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Item.jsx: sellItem with loader, blur and cleanup (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">async function sellItem() {\n  setBlur({ filter: &quot;blur(4px)&quot; });\n  setLoaderHidden(false);\n  const listingResult = await OPEND_backend.listItem(props.id, Number(price));\n  if (listingResult == &quot;Success&quot;) {\n    const openDId = await OPEND_backend.getOpenDCanisterID();\n    const transferResult = await NFTActor.transferOwnership(openDId);\n    if (transferResult == &quot;Success&quot;) {\n      setLoaderHidden(true);\n      setButton();\n      setPriceInput();\n      setOwner(&quot;OpenD&quot;);\n      setSellStatus(&quot;Listed&quot;);\n    }\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Item.jsx: check listing status on load</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const nftIsListed = await OPEND_backend.isListed(props.id);\nif (nftIsListed) {\n  setOwner(&quot;OpenD&quot;);\n  setBlur({ filter: &quot;blur(4px)&quot; });\n  setSellStatus(&quot;Listed&quot;);\n} else {\n  setButton(&lt;Button handleClick={handleSell} text={&quot;Sell&quot;} /&gt;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Style and persist the listed state of an NFT card</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Item.jsx from the previous lesson, where selling works but the UI does not change.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> After selling, the card shows a loader then a blurred image, owner OpenD, a Listed tag and no controls, and the same appearance survives a page refresh.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Paste the loader markup under the image and bind hidden to loaderHidden state (true initially).</li><li>In sellItem show the loader first, and after a successful transfer hide it, clear button and priceInput.</li><li>Set the owner text to OpenD after the transfer.</li><li>Add blur state and apply it as the image's style; set filter blur(4px) at the start of sellItem.</li><li>Add isListed to main.mo, redeploy, and call it in loadNFT to decide between listed styling and the Sell button.</li><li>Add sellStatus state and render it in the purple-text span; set to Listed in both places.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Selling shows a spinner, then a blurred card labelled Listed with owner OpenD; refreshing keeps that look.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to redeploy or to include isListed in the interface means the frontend call fails.</li><li>Redeploying wipes minted NFTs, so mint new ones to test.</li><li>Trying to sell an NFT already transferred to OpenD fails because the caller no longer owns it.</li><li>Without the isListed check the styling disappears on refresh.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l366", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds the Discover route by reusing the Gallery component with the IDs of listed NFTs, adds a role prop so Item renders differently for the collection and discover views, hides the Buy button from the original owner via a getOriginalOwner query, shows prices with a PriceLabel component, and uses dfx command-line calls to create a listing owned by someone else for testing.</p>\n<h3>Backend: getListedNFTs</h3>\n<ul><li>Add public query func getListedNFTs() : async [Principal] next to getOwnedNFTs in main.mo. No parameters.</li><li>HashMap.keys() returns an Iter of all keys; convert it with Iter.toArray, which needs import Iter from mo:base/Iter. The same Iter helpers were used earlier when persisting HashMaps.</li><li>Store the array in a constant (ids) and return it. The listing map's keys are exactly the listed NFTs' Principals.</li></ul>\n<h3>Header.jsx: a second gallery</h3>\n<ul><li>The Discover route currently renders just a placeholder heading. Replace it with a listingGallery state value, mirroring the userOwnedGallery used for the collection route.</li><li>Inside getNFTs (called once from useEffect) add const listedNFTIds = await OPEND_backend.getListedNFTs(), log it, then setListingGallery(&lt;Gallery title=&quot;Discover&quot; ids={listedNFTIds} role=&quot;discover&quot; /&gt;).</li><li>Because Gallery already maps ids to Item components, the same tile layout is reused with different data.</li><li>After redeploying, confirm the generated opend.did lists the new method. Mint two NFTs and list only one; Discover should show only the listed one while My NFTs shows both.</li></ul>\n<h3>The role prop</h3>\n<ul><li>Discover items must not be blurred and should offer Buy instead of Sell, so Item needs to know where it is rendered.</li><li>Pass role=&quot;collection&quot; from the My NFTs gallery and role=&quot;discover&quot; from the Discover gallery. Gallery forwards it with role={props.role} to each Item.</li><li>In loadNFT, the sell-related logic (listing check, blur, owner label, Sell button) moves under if (props.role == &quot;collection&quot;). An else if (props.role == &quot;discover&quot;) branch handles the marketplace view. Strings must match exactly between Header and Item; a typo silently skips both branches.</li><li>For discover, the button is a Button with text Buy and handleClick={handleBuy}; handleBuy is an async function that only logs a message for now (real buying comes in the next lecture).</li></ul>\n<h3>Hiding Buy from the original owner</h3>\n<ul><li>Problem: the user who listed an NFT sees a Buy button on it in Discover. The NFT's current owner is the OpenD canister, so the original seller is only recorded in the listing's itemOwner field.</li><li>Add public query func getOriginalOwner(id : Principal) : async Principal. Look up the listing with switch on mapOfListings.get(id): case null returns an empty Principal, case (?result) continues, then return listing.itemOwner.</li><li>In the discover branch, call await OPEND_backend.getOriginalOwner(props.id) and only set the Buy button when originalOwner.toText() != CURRENT_USER_ID.toText(). Comparing as text avoids comparing two different object instances.</li><li>CURRENT_USER_ID is exported from main.jsx (a Principal built with Principal.fromText of the default anonymous-style ID), so no login flow or deployed identity is needed. Import it into Item.jsx (and Header) from ../main.</li><li>Save main.mo before deploying; an unsaved file shows in italics in the editor.</li></ul>\n<h3>Testing a Buy button with a command-line NFT</h3>\n<ul><li>Everything minted from the UI is owned by the same identity, so the Buy button can never appear in practice. Mint from the terminal instead, using the dfx caller identity, which differs from the frontend's CURRENT_USER_ID.</li><li>README section 'Creating NFT for Testing' has four numbered commands: (1) mint an NFT through dfx canister call, which returns the new NFT's canister ID; (2) call listItem with that ID (quoted, no angle brackets) and a price; (3) get OpenD's ID with dfx canister id; (4) call the new NFT canister's transferOwnership, passing the OpenD ID.</li><li>Placeholders in angle brackets must be replaced entirely, and quote handling matters: the Principal arguments to Candid calls are quoted, while the canister name used as the call target is not. Each step should print Success (the first prints an ID).</li><li>After refreshing Discover, both listed items appear but only the command-line one has a Buy button.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> This works because the shell identity's principal is stored as itemOwner for the command-line listing, which differs from the browser's fixed ID.</li></ul>\n<h3>Price label</h3>\n<ul><li>Create components/PriceLabel.jsx exporting a functional component that returns the README's label markup and renders {props.sellPrice} in a span; the reference version appends the currency text DANG.</li><li>Add priceLabel state in Item.jsx and render {priceLabel} directly above the h2 title.</li><li>Backend: public query func getListedNFTPrice(id : Principal) : async Nat. Use the same switch on the listings map; return 0 for null, otherwise listing.itemPrice.</li><li>In the discover branch call await OPEND_backend.getListedNFTPrice(props.id) and setPriceLabel(&lt;PriceLabel sellPrice={price.toString()} /&gt;). The returned Nat arrives as a BigInt in JS, so convert with toString() before rendering.</li><li>Redeploy, mint and list a new NFT, and the price shows on the Discover tile.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo additions (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Iter &quot;mo:base/Iter&quot;;\n\npublic query func getListedNFTs() : async [Principal] {\n  let ids = Iter.toArray(mapOfListings.keys());\n  return ids;\n};\n\npublic query func getOriginalOwner(id : Principal) : async Principal {\n  var listing : Listing = switch (mapOfListings.get(id)) {\n    case null return Principal.fromText(&quot;&quot;);\n    case (?result) result;\n  };\n  return listing.itemOwner;\n};\n\npublic query func getListedNFTPrice(id : Principal) : async Nat {\n  var listing : Listing = switch (mapOfListings.get(id)) {\n    case null return 0;\n    case (?result) result;\n  };\n  return listing.itemPrice;\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Header.jsx: fetching both galleries</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">async function getNFTs() {\n  const userNFTIds = await OPEND_backend.getOwnedNFTs(CURRENT_USER_ID);\n  setOwnedGallery(&lt;Gallery title=&quot;My NFTs&quot; ids={userNFTIds} role=&quot;collection&quot; /&gt;);\n\n  const listedNFTIds = await OPEND_backend.getListedNFTs();\n  setListingGallery(&lt;Gallery title=&quot;Discover&quot; ids={listedNFTIds} role=&quot;discover&quot; /&gt;);\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Item.jsx loadNFT: role-based branches</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">if (props.role == &quot;collection&quot;) {\n  const nftIsListed = await OPEND_backend.isListed(props.id);\n  if (nftIsListed) {\n    setOwner(&quot;OpenD&quot;);\n    setBlur({ filter: &quot;blur(4px)&quot; });\n    setSellStatus(&quot;Listed&quot;);\n  } else {\n    setButton(&lt;Button handleClick={handleSell} text={&quot;Sell&quot;} /&gt;);\n  }\n} else if (props.role == &quot;discover&quot;) {\n  const originalOwner = await OPEND_backend.getOriginalOwner(props.id);\n  if (originalOwner.toText() != CURRENT_USER_ID.toText()) {\n    setButton(&lt;Button handleClick={handleBuy} text={&quot;Buy&quot;} /&gt;);\n  }\n  const price = await OPEND_backend.getListedNFTPrice(props.id);\n  setPriceLabel(&lt;PriceLabel sellPrice={price.toString()} /&gt;);\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Gallery.jsx: forward role to each Item</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">props.ids.map((NFTId) =&gt; (\n  &lt;Item id={NFTId} key={NFTId.toText()} role={props.role} /&gt;\n))</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">PriceLabel.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction PriceLabel(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;disButtonBase-root disChip-root makeStyles-price-23 disChip-outlined&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;span className=&quot;disChip-label&quot;&gt;{props.sellPrice} DANG&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default PriceLabel;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Build the Discover page with Buy buttons and prices</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project after lesson 42.07: listing works, Item has loader/blur/Listed styling, Discover route shows only a placeholder heading.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Discover lists all NFTs for sale with their price, shows Buy only on NFTs originally owned by someone else, and My NFTs still shows the Sell flow.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add getListedNFTs (with Iter import) to main.mo and fetch it in Header.getNFTs into a listingGallery state rendered in the /discover route.</li><li>Pass role=&quot;collection&quot; and role=&quot;discover&quot; to the two Gallery elements and forward it to Item.</li><li>Wrap the existing sell logic of loadNFT in the collection branch and add a discover branch showing a Buy button wired to a logging handleBuy.</li><li>Add getOriginalOwner and show Buy only when the original owner's text differs from CURRENT_USER_ID's text.</li><li>Redeploy and use the README's command-line steps to mint, list and transfer a test NFT to OpenD from a different identity.</li><li>Create PriceLabel.jsx, render it above the title, add getListedNFTPrice, and set the label from its result via toString().</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Discover shows listed NFTs unblurred with price tags; the self-listed NFT has no Buy button, the command-line one does.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mismatched role strings between Header and Item silently render no button.</li><li>Leaving angle brackets, or adding/removing quotes, in the README dfx commands breaks the test NFT setup.</li><li>Principals compared as objects are never equal; compare toText() results.</li><li>Passing a BigInt Nat to a component without toString() fails to render.</li><li>Redeploying clears everything, so re-mint after each backend change.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The 42.07 reference repo already contains getListedNFTs, getOriginalOwner and getListedNFTPrice in main.mo, so its main.mo is identical to 42.08's; the README write-up of this lesson shows a simpler PriceLabel snippet than the repo's component.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l367", "<p class=\"summary\">Final step of the OpenD NFT marketplace: the Buy button now pays the seller in DANG tokens by calling a separate token canister, then asks the OpenD backend to move NFT ownership to the buyer. Also adds a loader and hides the bought item from the Discover page.</p>\n<h3>Overall purchase flow</h3>\n<ul><li>Buying has three parts: (1) take the price in DANG from the buyer's token balance and credit the seller, (2) change the NFT's owner to the buyer, (3) tidy the marketplace records (remove the listing, update ownership lists).</li><li>Part 1 uses the token canister built in an earlier module. This is the first time one canister (OpenD, via the frontend) talks to another canister, which is the main new idea of the lecture.</li><li>The frontend orchestrates: it calls token.transfer first, and only if the result is the text 'Success' does it call the new OpenD method completePurchase.</li></ul>\n<h3>Getting the token project running (token-local)</h3>\n<ul><li>To make everyone use the same token code, a zipped 'token-local' project is provided as a lesson download. Unzip it and drop the folder next to the other ic-projects, then open it in its own VS Code window.</li><li>It contains a token backend canister (tracks balances, lets you query a balance, pay out from a faucet, and transfer to another principal) and a small token frontend.</li><li>Follow the project's README deploy section: get your own principal from the command line (dfx identity get-principal), paste it as the owner value inside quotes in the token's main.mo, then run dfx deploy while dfx start is already running for OpenD.</li><li>Start its frontend with npm start. Because OpenD already occupies localhost:8080, the dev server picks the next port, localhost:8081.</li><li>Seed the token canister: store the token canister's id in a shell variable (the README gives the command, named CANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY), then call token transfer to send it half a billion tokens. Afterwards press the faucet button in the token frontend; the anonymous user receives 10,000 DANG, which is the balance used to buy NFTs.</li><li>Everything runs on a local replica with the anonymous identity (no Internet Identity login, no deploy to mainnet). This keeps the testing free; minting on the live network costs roughly several dollars of cycles each time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Sharing the token canister's declarations with OpenD</h3>\n<ul><li>To call another canister the caller needs its interface. Copy the token project's declarations folder for the token canister into OpenD's declarations folder, so it sits beside the nft and opend declarations. (For a third party's live canister, its owner would supply this.)</li><li>token.did.js exports an idlFactory describing which methods exist and their input/output types, exactly like the idlFactory used for the dynamically created NFT canisters.</li><li>Both factories are called idlFactory, so alias the second import: import { idlFactory as tokenIdlFactory } from the token declarations path. In Item.jsx this goes next to the existing nft idlFactory import.</li></ul>\n<h3>handleBuy in Item.jsx</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a token actor with Actor.createActor(tokenIdlFactory, { agent, canisterId }). It reuses the same HttpAgent and localhost host as the NFT actors. The canisterId is Principal.fromText('&lt;id string&gt;'), with the id obtained by running dfx canister id token inside the token-local project.</li><li>Get the seller with the existing OpenD query getOriginalOwner(props.id) (returns the Principal who listed the item) and the price with getListedNFTPrice(props.id) (returns a Nat). Both are awaited.</li><li>Call tokenActor.transfer(sellerId, itemPrice). Its two inputs are the destination principal and a natural-number amount, and it returns Text describing the outcome ('Success' when it worked). Log it while testing; checking the token frontend's balance should show it dropped by the price (2 DANG in the demo).</li><li>Wrap the follow-up in if (result == 'Success'): only then call the new backend method completePurchase with id (props.id), ownerId (the seller) and newOwnerId (CURRENT_USER_ID, the user identity exported by the app's main file). Await it and log the returned text.</li></ul>\n<h3>New backend function: completePurchase (main.mo)</h3>\n<ul><li>Signature: public shared (msg) func completePurchase(id : Principal, ownerId : Principal, newOwnerId : Principal) : async Text.</li><li>Step 1: look up the NFT canister in mapOfNFTs with a switch on mapOfNFTs.get(id). The result is optional, so case null returns 'NFT does not exist' and case (?result) unwraps it into purchasedNFT (type NFTActorClass.NFT). A temporary 'return Success' at the end can be used while building to silence compiler errors.</li><li>Step 2: call await purchasedNFT.transferOwnership(newOwnerId). That method already exists in nft.mo and takes the new owner; its Text result is stored as transferResult. It needs await because it is an inter-canister call; forgetting it gives a type error saying Text was found where async Text was expected.</li><li>Step 3, only when transferResult == 'Success': remove the listing with mapOfListings.delete(id). HashMap.delete removes the entry for the key and does nothing if the key is missing.</li><li>Step 4: fetch the previous owner's list of NFT ids from mapOfOwners.get(ownerId) with a switch (null gives an empty List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;(), otherwise unwrap), assign it to a var ownedNFTs.</li><li>Step 5: List.filter(ownedNFTs, func) builds a new list from only those elements for which the function returns true. The function receives each id (listItemId : Principal) and returns listItemId != id, so the purchased NFT is dropped. Assign the result back with :=.</li><li>Step 6: give the NFT to the new owner with the existing helper addToOwnershipMap(newOwnerId, id), called outside the filter function. Return 'Success'.</li><li>Otherwise (transfer failed) return transferResult itself so the caller sees the error text.</li><li>A typo in a parameter name (newOwner vs newOwnerId) caused one compile error in the lecture; read compiler messages closely.</li></ul>\n<h3>Redeploy and test sequence</h3>\n<ul><li>After changing main.mo, redeploy OpenD using the deploy command from the OpenD README.</li><li>The Discover page only shows listed NFTs, so mint one with the command-line mint command in the README, then list it for sale with the README's list command (paste in the new NFT principal), then transfer it to the OpenD canister (paste the OpenD canister id and the NFT principal into the README's final call). This produces an item for sale that the frontend user did not list themself.</li><li>Click Buy: the console shows the purchase result 'Success', the item vanishes from Discover after a refresh, appears under My NFTs, and the token frontend balance falls by the price.</li></ul>\n<h3>UI polish: loader and hiding the item</h3>\n<ul><li>At the start of handleBuy call setLoaderHidden(false) to show the loading animation; set it back to true when the purchase finishes.</li><li>To remove a bought item without a reload, add state: const [shouldDisplay, setDisplay] = useState(true).</li><li>On the root div of Item.jsx add style={{ display: shouldDisplay ? 'inline' : 'none' }}. display: none removes the element from view although it remains in the DOM (visible in dev tools); 'inline' is the item's normal value.</li><li>At the end of handleBuy call setDisplay(false), so the card disappears as soon as the purchase succeeds.</li><li>Demo: starting at 9,988 DANG, buying a 2 DANG item shows the loader, the card disappears, and the balance drops by 2.</li></ul>\n<h3>Wrap-up remarks</h3>\n<ul><li>The project now supports minting, listing, buying and viewing owned NFTs on the Internet Computer, mirroring the core of a marketplace like OpenSea.</li><li>Deploying live costs real cycles for every mint; the DFINITY developer grant program is mentioned as a way to get funding for a serious project.</li><li>Each earlier lesson ships the code for that step, which is handy when debugging; expect many errors in a Motoko plus React project.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Backend: completePurchase in main.mo (reference repo, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func completePurchase(id : Principal, ownerId : Principal, newOwnerId : Principal) : async Text {\n  var purchasedNFT : NFTActorClass.NFT = switch (mapOfNFTs.get(id)) {\n    case null return &quot;NFT does not exist&quot;;\n    case (?result) result;\n  };\n\n  let transferResult = await purchasedNFT.transferOwnership(newOwnerId);\n  if (transferResult == &quot;Success&quot;) {\n    mapOfListings.delete(id);\n    var ownedNFTs : List.List&lt;Principal&gt; = switch (mapOfOwners.get(ownerId)) {\n      case null List.nil&lt;Principal&gt;();\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    ownedNFTs := List.filter(\n      ownedNFTs,\n      func(listItemId : Principal) : Bool {\n        return listItemId != id;\n      },\n    );\n\n    addToOwnershipMap(newOwnerId, id);\n    return &quot;Success&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return transferResult;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Frontend: imports and handleBuy in Item.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { idlFactory } from &quot;../../../declarations/nft&quot;;\nimport { idlFactory as tokenIdlFactory } from &quot;../../../declarations/token&quot;;\nimport { Principal } from &quot;@dfinity/principal&quot;;\n\nasync function handleBuy() {\n  setLoaderHidden(false);\n  const tokenActor = await Actor.createActor(tokenIdlFactory, {\n    agent,\n    canisterId: Principal.fromText(&quot;&lt;TOKEN CANISTER ID from dfx canister id token&gt;&quot;),\n  });\n\n  const sellerId = await OPEND_backend.getOriginalOwner(props.id);\n  const itemPrice = await OPEND_backend.getListedNFTPrice(props.id);\n\n  const result = await tokenActor.transfer(sellerId, itemPrice);\n  if (result == &quot;Success&quot;) {\n    const transferResult = await OPEND_backend.completePurchase(\n      props.id,\n      sellerId,\n      CURRENT_USER_ID\n    );\n    console.log(&quot;purchase: &quot; + transferResult);\n    setLoaderHidden(true);\n    setDisplay(false);\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Hiding the card after purchase</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const [shouldDisplay, setDisplay] = useState(true);\n// ...\n&lt;div\n  style={{ display: shouldDisplay ? &quot;inline&quot; : &quot;none&quot; }}\n  className=&quot;disGrid-item&quot;\n&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Token canister setup commands (shape only; exact lines come from the token-local README)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx identity get-principal          # copy into token main.mo as the owner\ndfx deploy                          # in token-local, with dfx start running\nnpm start                           # token frontend appears on localhost:8081\ndfx canister id token               # id used in Principal.fromText(...)</pre>\n<h2>The task: Enable buying NFTs with DANG tokens</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The OpenD project from the previous lesson: Item.jsx has a handleBuy that only logs that buying was triggered; main.mo has getOriginalOwner, getListedNFTPrice, listItem and addToOwnershipMap. The separate token-local project is provided as a download.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clicking Buy transfers the price in DANG from the buyer to the seller, moves NFT ownership to the buyer, removes the listing, shows a loader meanwhile and hides the card from Discover afterwards.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download and unzip token-local, put it beside the other projects, open it in a new VS Code window.</li><li>Put your own principal (dfx identity get-principal) inside quotes as owner in the token main.mo, run dfx deploy, then npm start (port 8081).</li><li>Store the token canister id in a shell variable, transfer 500,000,000 tokens to it, then use the faucet button to give the frontend user 10,000 DANG.</li><li>Copy the token declarations folder into OpenD's declarations folder.</li><li>In Item.jsx import the token idlFactory under the alias tokenIdlFactory.</li><li>In handleBuy create tokenActor with Actor.createActor using that factory, the shared agent and Principal.fromText of the token canister id.</li><li>Await getOriginalOwner and getListedNFTPrice for props.id, then await tokenActor.transfer(sellerId, itemPrice) and log the result.</li><li>Add completePurchase to main.mo: unwrap the NFT, call transferOwnership, delete the listing, filter the old owner's list, add the NFT to the new owner, return Success or the failure text.</li><li>In handleBuy, when the token result equals 'Success', await OPEND_backend.completePurchase(props.id, sellerId, CURRENT_USER_ID) and log it.</li><li>Redeploy OpenD, mint and list an NFT with the README commands, transfer it to the OpenD canister, then click Buy and verify.</li><li>Add the loader toggling and the shouldDisplay state with the conditional display style.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Buy shows the loader, the console logs the purchase result Success, the card disappears from Discover, the NFT appears in My NFTs for the buyer, and the token balance is reduced by the item price.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">// See code_examples: completePurchase (main.mo) and handleBuy (Item.jsx).</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Missing await on the inter-canister call gives a type error (Text found where async Text expected).</li><li>Parameter name typos (newOwner vs newOwnerId) cause compile errors.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In both the lecture code and the reference repo, the filtered ownedNFTs list is never written back with mapOfOwners.put(ownerId, ownedNFTs), so the seller's stored list still contains the sold NFT. Add that put line to fix it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The token payment and the ownership change are two separate calls from the browser. If the second fails, the buyer has paid without receiving the NFT, and a malicious client could skip the check. A production design would do the payment inside the backend.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's Item.jsx leaves a placeholder for the token canister id; it must be replaced with the real id from dfx canister id token, and that id changes if the token project is redeployed fresh.</li><li>Port clash: OpenD uses 8080, so the token frontend runs on 8081; don't mix them up.</li><li>Item to buy must be listed by someone other than the current user; mint and list one with the command line first.</li><li>Copying declarations: the token declarations must be re-copied if the token canister's interface changes.</li><li>Real-network minting costs money, so keep testing on localhost.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l368", "<p class=\"summary\">Promotional article closing the Internet Computer section: it points learners to a 2022 global hackathon run by the Internet Computer ecosystem. No code or exercise.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Announcement for a global Internet Computer hackathon (10 May to 20 June 2022) with more than $6 million in prizes and grants, aimed at learners who now know Motoko and ICP development.</li><li>Contains a registration link and asks participants to share their projects with the instructor on Twitter.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The event dates are long past, so treat this as an admin or marketing item; the useful idea is that DFINITY grants and hackathons exist for ICP projects.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Content taken from the reference repo's README for this lesson, because the lecture page in the browser tab showed only an Open button rather than the article body.</li></ul>");
